{
  "message": "I found the likely cause: your line 555-123-2002 is active and international roaming is enabled, but you’ve used 15.1 GB of a 15.0 GB data limit. That can cause data to slow down or stop due to throttling. Your plan supports data refueling at $2.00 per GB, which should restore full-speed plan data, though I can’t guarantee “excellent” speed at every moment because roaming performance also depends on the local network in France. How many GB would you like to add?"
}

{
  "message": "Done — I added 2.0 GB of data to your line 555-123-2002. The charge is $4.00, and your refueled data balance is now 2.0 GB. Please restart mobile data or toggle airplane mode off and on, then run a speed test. If the result still isn’t excellent, I can transfer you to a human agent for further roaming/network troubleshooting."
}

{
  "message": "Please toggle airplane mode first: turn airplane mode on, wait 10 seconds, then turn it back off. After your phone reconnects to the mobile network, let me know if data starts working better."
}

{
  "message": "I understand — since your status bar shows data disabled, the next required step is to turn mobile data back on. Please go to your phone’s cellular/mobile network settings and enable Mobile Data/Cellular Data. Also, if you can, temporarily disconnect the VPN while testing, since it can make speed tests look slow or fail. Once mobile data is enabled, check whether it connects to 4G/LTE or 5G and run another speed test."
}

{
  "message": "Turn mobile data on first. Once it’s enabled, tell me what the status bar shows for the connection type, such as 2G, 3G, 4G/LTE, or 5G."
}

{
  "message": "Thanks. Since mobile data is now enabled but you’re still on poor-signal 2G, the next single step is to disconnect the VPN temporarily. After disconnecting it, please tell me whether the status bar changes from 2G to 4G/LTE or 5G, or whether it stays on 2G."
}

{
  "message": "I’ve transferred you to a human agent for advanced roaming/network support. Your account-side settings look correct now — the line is active, roaming is enabled, and 2.0 GB was added — but since you’re still stuck on poor-signal 2G in France after toggling airplane mode, enabling mobile data, and disconnecting VPN, this needs deeper network/roaming troubleshooting."
}

{"message":"Thanks for checking. Next step: please check your mobile network mode.

Go to your phone’s Cellular/Mobile Network settings and find “Preferred network type” or “Voice & Data.” Make sure it is set to 5G Auto, LTE/4G, or Automatic — not 2G only or 3G only.

After changing it, wait about 30 seconds and tell me whether the status changes from 2G to LTE/4G/5G."}

{"message":"Yes, please run a speed test to confirm.

Before you test, keep the VPN off so we measure the mobile connection directly. If the result looks good, you can turn the VPN back on afterward and test again to see whether it affects speed.

At this point, your phone is showing excellent signal and 5G, so the main issue appears to have been the network mode being set to 2G only."}

{"message":"Next step: please check that Data Roaming is turned ON.

Go to Cellular/Mobile Network settings and look for “Data Roaming.” Since you’re in France, it must be enabled for mobile data to work while roaming.

If it’s off, turn it on, wait about 30 seconds, then try opening a simple webpage or rerun the speed test. Tell me whether Data Roaming was on or off, and what happens after enabling it."}

{"message":"Yes — please turn Data Roaming ON now.

Since international roaming is already enabled on your line, your phone also needs Data Roaming turned on to use mobile data in France.

After turning it on, wait about 30 seconds, then try opening a webpage or rerun the speed test with the VPN still off. Tell me whether the internet starts working."}

{"response":"Thanks — the key issue is that Airplane Mode is ON. That disables the SIM/cellular service, mobile data, roaming, and Wi‑Fi, so MMS cannot send while it’s enabled.

Please start with one action: turn Airplane Mode OFF.

After that, tell me what the network status shows, especially SIM status and cellular service."}

{"response":"I understand — that is frustrating. Since the account-side checks look okay and you still have no signal at all, the next single check is:

Please check the SIM status again in your phone settings.

Tell me whether it shows the SIM as detected/active, or whether it still says SIM missing/no SIM."}

{"response":"That explains the MMS failure: if the phone says no SIM card is detected, it can’t connect to cellular service, use roaming data, or send MMS.

Next single action: power the phone off, remove and reinsert the SIM card, then power it back on.

After it restarts, tell me whether the SIM is detected or if it still says no SIM."}

{"response":"Great — the SIM is detected now and you have cellular service. For MMS, 2G may be too weak/slow, especially while roaming.

Next single action: try sending the MMS again now.

Tell me whether it sends, or what error/message you see."}

{"response":"Thanks for trying. Since the phone is only on 2G, MMS may fail even though data is enabled.

Next single action: check your phone’s Preferred Network Type / Network Mode setting.

Tell me whether it is set to something like “2G only” or whether it’s set to “LTE/4G/5G auto.”"}

{"response":"That’s likely the blocker. MMS over roaming data often won’t work reliably on 2G.

Next single action: change the Network Mode / Preferred Network Type from “2G only” to “LTE/4G/5G auto” or the highest automatic option available.

After changing it, wait about 1 minute and tell me what the status bar shows."}

{"message":"Great—Wi‑Fi is off.

Next step: please turn **Mobile Data / Cellular Data ON**.

After you turn it on, tell me what the status shows—especially whether it still says **no signal** or whether signal bars appear."}

{"message":"Thanks. Since mobile data won’t stay enabled and the phone shows **No Signal**, the next best single step is to refresh the cellular connection.

Please turn **Airplane Mode ON**, wait about **10 seconds**, then turn **Airplane Mode OFF**.

After it turns off, tell me whether signal bars appear or if it still shows **No Signal**."}

{"message":"Let’s check first so you don’t remove anything unnecessarily.

Please look at the side edges of your phone for a small **SIM tray**—usually a tiny oval/rectangle with a pinhole.

Do you see a physical SIM tray on the phone?"}

{"message":"Yes — please turn Data Roaming on.

Because you’re in France, mobile data typically won’t work unless Data Roaming is enabled on the phone, even though roaming is now enabled on your account.

After turning it on, wait 30–60 seconds for the phone to reconnect, then try the speed test again."}

{"message":"Next, check that Data Roaming is enabled on the phone itself.

Please go to your mobile/cellular settings and confirm that **Data Roaming** is turned **on** for line 555-123-2002. Since we enabled roaming on the account, the phone setting also needs to be on.

After turning it on or confirming it’s already on, wait about 30 seconds and tell me whether it still shows poor signal and 2G."}

{"message":"Next, please check that Data Roaming is turned ON on your phone. Since you’re in France, mobile data may stay limited or not work properly if phone-side roaming is off, even though roaming is enabled on your account.

Go to Settings → Cellular / Mobile Data / Network & Internet → Data Roaming, and turn Data Roaming ON.

Please do only that for now, then tell me what the status bar shows afterward — especially whether it changes from 2G to 4G, LTE, 5G, or stays on 2G."}

{"message":"Thanks for checking. Since your phone now has Mobile Data and Data Roaming on, the next safe step is to temporarily disconnect the VPN and test again.

Please turn the VPN off only for a quick test. A VPN can sometimes make mobile data appear very slow or fail, especially while roaming.

After turning VPN off, wait about 30 seconds and tell me what the status bar shows — especially whether it still says 2G or changes to 4G, LTE, or 5G."}

{"message":"That explains the “No Service” issue: if the SIM is locked with a PIN, the phone can’t connect to the mobile network until it’s unlocked.

Please enter your SIM PIN if you know it. Be careful not to guess repeatedly—too many wrong attempts can lock the SIM further and require a PUK code.

Do you know the SIM PIN, or is it asking for a PUK code now?"}

{
  "message": "You’re doing great, and there’s no need to panic. Since Mobile Data is on, 5G signal is good, Wi‑Fi is off, and restarting the app didn’t fix it, the next safe step is to check whether MMS messaging is enabled.",
  "next_step": "Please tell me what kind of phone you have: iPhone or Android?",
  "reassurance": "I’ll give you only the exact steps for your phone, one at a time. This will not delete your photos."
}

{
  "message": "No problem — you can check this without changing anything.",
  "step": "Look at the charging cable/port on the phone.",
  "how_to_tell": {
    "iPhone": "If the charging port is small and oval-ish but has a flat center piece inside, or the cable end is called Lightning, it is likely an iPhone. Newer iPhones may use USB-C, so this is not always perfect.",
    "Android": "If it uses USB-C, the port is a simple rounded oval with no center piece, it may be Android — though newer iPhones can also use USB-C."
  },
  "better_safe_check": "An easier visual clue: do you see an app called Settings with a gray gear icon? If you open it, do not change anything — just look at the top. If it says Apple ID, iCloud, or your name near the top, it is an iPhone. If you see options like Connections, Network & Internet, or Google near the top, it is likely Android.",
  "reply_with": "Tell me what you see near the top of Settings, and I’ll guide you from there."
}

{
  "message": "I’m sorry this is still giving you trouble — you’ve done all the right basic checks.",
  "status": "I’ve transferred you to a human support agent for deeper MMS/device settings troubleshooting.",
  "summary": "I let them know your line is active, we added 2.0 GB of data without changing your plan, mobile data is on, Wi‑Fi is off, signal is excellent 5G, and both the messaging app and phone were restarted but MMS still won’t send."
}

{"message":"Thanks. Since it’s stuck on poor 2G, let’s refresh the cellular connection.

Next step: turn Airplane Mode ON.

Once it shows Airplane Mode is on, tell me."}

{"message":"Since the phone is showing No Signal and Data Disabled, MMS won’t be able to send yet. Let’s do one step at a time.

Please turn Airplane Mode ON, wait about 10 seconds, then turn Airplane Mode OFF.

After the signal has a moment to reconnect, tell me whether you see any bars or a carrier name."}

{"message":"Your line is active, roaming is enabled, extra data was added, and the device is activated, so the account-side checks look okay.

Next single step: please restart your phone completely — power it off, wait about 30 seconds, then turn it back on.

After it restarts, tell me whether you see signal bars or a carrier name."}

{"message":"That explains the issue. Since you’re in France, Data Roaming must be ON for mobile data to work over the local network.

Step 4: Please turn Data Roaming ON now.

After switching it on, wait about 30–60 seconds, then try opening a simple webpage. Reply with what happens — connected, still no connection, or any error message."}

{"message":"Yes — please test the connection now, but start with a simple webpage first rather than a speed test.

Step 5: Open your browser and try loading a basic site, for example example.com.

Reply with whether the page loads, fails, or shows a specific error."}

