}

{
  "message": "Yes. First, please check the current preferred network mode only. Tell me exactly what it is set to now — for example, 5G Auto, LTE/4G, 3G, 2G/GSM, or Global/Automatic."
}

{"message":"You’re okay — please don’t worry. “Data disabled” will not affect your photos, and it usually just means the phone isn’t connected to the cellular network right now.

Let’s do just one small thing next so I can guide you correctly:

Is your phone an iPhone, or is it an Android phone like Samsung, Motorola, or Google Pixel?"}

{"message":"Yes. Look at the back of the phone or the case, if you can see it.

If you see an **Apple logo** — the shape like an apple with a bite taken out — it’s an **iPhone**.

If you do **not** see an Apple logo, tell me what name or logo you do see, if any."}

{"message":"Thank you — that helps. “SIM card status is missing” usually means the iPhone is not detecting the SIM/eSIM properly, which would explain the **No Service** message.

Your device is eSIM-capable and appears activated on the account, so the next step is deeper SIM/eSIM provisioning troubleshooting that I can’t complete with the available tools here.

I can transfer you to a human support agent now and include that your line was resumed successfully but the iPhone still shows **No Service** and **SIM card status missing**. Would you like me to transfer you?"}

{"message":"Let’s address the SIM/no-service issue first. If the phone says the SIM card is missing, data roaming won’t help yet because the phone isn’t connected to the cellular network at all.

Step 1: Please restart your phone completely.

After it powers back on, wait about 60 seconds, then check the status bar and SIM status again. Tell me whether it still says SIM missing/no service, or whether you now see signal bars, a carrier name, R, 4G, LTE, or 5G."}

{"message":"Thanks for trying that. Since the line is active, roaming is enabled on the account, and you now have extra data, the next thing to check is the phone’s device-level roaming setting.

Next single step: please check whether **Data Roaming** is turned on in your phone’s cellular/mobile network settings.

If it’s off, turn **Data Roaming on**. Then tell me whether the status bar changes from “no signal” or “data disabled.”"}

{"message":"Address the **missing SIM** first. If the phone can’t detect the SIM, turning Data Roaming on won’t help because the phone has no cellular line to connect with.

Next single step: please power the phone off, remove and reinsert the SIM card securely, then power it back on.

If you’re using an eSIM instead of a physical SIM, check that the eSIM line is turned on in cellular settings. Let me know whether the SIM status still says missing after that."}

{"message":"Good — the SIM is being detected now, so we’ve made progress.

Next single step: turn **Data Roaming on** in your cellular/mobile network settings.

After that, wait 30–60 seconds and tell me whether the status changes from **2G/data disabled** to something like **3G/4G/LTE/5G** or whether it still shows data disabled."}

{"message":"Thanks — roaming is on now, but mobile data is still disabled.

Next single step: turn **Mobile Data / Cellular Data** on again now that the SIM is detected and Data Roaming is on.

After that, wait 30–60 seconds and tell me whether the status still says **data disabled** or changes to a data indicator like **3G/4G/LTE**."}

{"message":"Great — data is enabled now, so the remaining issue is that you’re only connected on **poor 2G**, which often cannot send MMS reliably.

Next single step: please set your phone’s **Network Selection** to **Automatic** or set **Preferred Network Type** to **4G/LTE/5G auto** if you see that option.

Then wait 1–2 minutes and tell me whether the status changes from 2G to 3G/4G/LTE/5G."}

{"message":"Excellent — you now have strong 5G service with data enabled, which should allow MMS to send.

Next single step: try sending the MMS again from your messaging app.

Let me know whether it sends successfully."}

{"message":"Thanks for checking. Since you now have strong 5G and data enabled, the next step is to confirm whether mobile data itself is working outside the messaging app.

Next single step: turn Wi‑Fi off, keep mobile data on, and try opening a website in your browser.

Tell me whether the website loads successfully over mobile data."}
